        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Remember that the exponential function <span class=\"math\">[MATH: e^x]</span> has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn&#x27;t change anything, and we have\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right)  = e^ x.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Remember that the exponential function [MATH: e^x] has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn't change anything, and we have\n[MATH: \\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right) = e^ x.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Since integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Since integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n[MATH: \\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nRemember that the exponential function [MATH: e^x] has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn't change anything, and we have\n[MATH: \\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right) = e^ x.]\n\nContent:\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n[MATH: \\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C.]"
      },
